            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Carter G. Phillips, in 1995 Congress changed securities\u2011law pleading rules, rejecting the traditional notice\u2011pleading standard and requiring complaints to state facts that give rise to a strong inference of the defendant\u2019s intent."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The petitioner argues that Congress also eliminated the rule that courts must interpret pleadings in the light most favorable to the plaintiff."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d939ed6653e4-0001-0006"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 26,
              "start": 26
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The petitioner says the required scienter is either intent or recklessness."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d939ed6653e40bf096f9ba9f67ecd3f5e345bc6f3c37a2d6de104fb5b3324c19",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d939ed6653e4-0002-0011"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 55,
              "start": 55
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Carter G. Phillips (Petitioner), according to Carter G. Phillips (Petitioner), the petitioner contends that its approach is consistent with prior Supreme Court decisions in Matsushita and Monsanto, providing guidance for lower courts."
      }
    ],
